
PROPERTY RIGHTS FOR ME, TRAINING DATA FOR THEE

Data Appropriation, Regulatory Moats, and the Infrastructure Protection Paradox of Generative AI

Subject: AI Governance / Intellectual Property / Competition Policy / Satirical Political Economy
Date: October 2026

⸻

Abstract

Generative artificial intelligence has produced an unusual theory of property.

When books, journalism, illustrations, software, photographs, websites, game assets and other human-created works are available on the open internet, AI developers frequently describe large-scale ingestion of those materials using terms such as training, learning, scraping, transformation and fair use. When the resulting AI systems themselves become economically valuable, however, the vocabulary changes abruptly. Copying becomes model extraction. Learning from outputs becomes distillation attack. Automated collection becomes unauthorised extraction. Reverse engineering becomes prohibited. Infrastructure becomes strategically sensitive. Model weights become national-security assets.

This paper calls this asymmetry the Infrastructure Protection Paradox:

The AI industry developed by treating enormous quantities of other people’s informational property as raw material while increasingly demanding strong contractual, technological and governmental protection against other actors treating AI systems in the same way.

The argument is not that AI companies possess no legal rights. Nor does it assume that every instance of AI training constitutes copyright infringement; that question remains contested and jurisdiction-specific. The narrower and stronger claim is one of reciprocity and legitimacy. If large-scale appropriation is justified by innovation, transformation and learning when performed by frontier laboratories, a principled explanation is required for why analogous extraction becomes unacceptable once the laboratory owns the valuable object.

The tension becomes particularly acute as frontier laboratories simultaneously advocate mandatory AI regulation that, whatever its safety rationale, imposes fixed compliance costs that established firms are unusually well positioned to absorb.

The result is a curious property regime:

everything is potentially training data until it becomes ours.
Once it becomes ours, please call Congress.

