1. Introduction: The One-Way Property Regime

Generative AI did not emerge in an informational vacuum.

Modern foundation models were developed using extraordinary quantities of text, images, code and other digital material. The UK Government’s 2026 copyright report states plainly that AI developers and dataset suppliers have used web crawlers to scrape large volumes of online content and that a significant proportion of scraped material is protected by copyright and related rights. It also notes that developers often provide limited disclosure of the works used, making it difficult for rights holders even to determine whether their material entered a training dataset. 

This is an extraordinary starting position for an industry increasingly concerned about the sanctity of its own intellectual assets.

OpenAI’s current terms prohibit customers from reverse-engineering its systems, programmatically extracting data or outputs, and using outputs to develop competing models. Its business agreement goes further and expressly defines model extraction and “stealing attacks” as prohibited reverse engineering. 

Anthropic likewise publicly describes large-scale competitor distillation as “illicit” when rivals use Claude outputs to improve their models. Anthropic states that DeepSeek, Moonshot and MiniMax generated more than 16 million Claude exchanges through roughly 24,000 accounts in alleged violation of its terms and regional restrictions. Intriguingly, Anthropic also acknowledges that distillation itself is a legitimate and widely used training method when laboratories perform it on their own models. 

The resulting distinction is elegant:

When we learn from your work, it is machine learning.
When you learn from our machine, it is model theft.

This paper asks whether that distinction can be defended as a coherent principle rather than merely as ownership wearing a lab coat.

