2. Terminology: “Theft” Is Rhetorical; Copying Is Legally Complicated

Before proceeding, one distinction matters.

“Data theft” is used in this paper as a description of the political-economic grievance: valuable works being copied or consumed without the creator’s permission or remuneration.

It is not a claim that every act of web scraping legally constitutes criminal theft.

Copyright law is more complicated. Different jurisdictions have different exceptions; US fair-use doctrine is fact-specific; UK law currently remains contested in relation to AI training; contractual rights, database rights, technological protection measures and lawful access introduce further distinctions.

The UK Government said in March 2026 that the application of existing copyright law to AI training remains disputed and expressly declined to rush reform while evidence was still developing. 

US courts are also drawing lines rather than announcing a universal exemption. In the Anthropic litigation, a court treated training on lawfully obtained books as fair use while distinguishing the company’s acquisition and retention of pirated copies; Anthropic ultimately reached a $1.5 billion settlement with authors concerning pirated books. 

Separately, the Third Circuit has now upheld Thomson Reuters’ copyright victory against Ross Intelligence over copying Westlaw headnotes to develop an AI legal-search product. That case is not directly controlling for generative LLMs, but it demonstrates that adding the words AI training does not automatically transform copying into fair use. 

Meanwhile, the consolidated New York litigation against OpenAI and Microsoft remains a major unresolved test of whether unauthorised use of books and journalism for generative-model training is protected as transformative fair use. 

The defensible statement is therefore not:

“Every AI company has been legally found guilty of stealing everything.”

It is:

“The industry built models using vast quantities of third-party material under legal theories that remain heavily contested, while simultaneously constructing extremely strong legal and contractual barriers around extraction from its own products.”

That asymmetry exists regardless of how the pending copyright cases ultimately resolve.

