3. The Vocabulary Transformation Machine

Consider the linguistic transformation that occurs when ownership changes.

Activity	When performed on creators’ material	When performed on frontier models
Automated collection	Scraping	Unauthorised extraction
Learning statistical structure	Training	Capability theft
Using examples to improve a system	Machine learning	Distillation attack
Studying functionality	Research	Reverse engineering
Replicating useful behaviour	Transformation	Competitive model extraction
Copying at immense scale	Dataset creation	Security incident

This is not merely satire.

OpenAI contracts expressly forbid using outputs to build competing AI models, extracting service data outside permitted methods, and attempting to discover underlying components of its models and systems. 

Anthropic’s February 2026 statement similarly objects that competitors can acquire capabilities far more cheaply than developing them independently by training on Claude outputs. 

That sentence deserves attention.

Because it is exceptionally close to the economic complaint made by writers, artists, programmers and publishers:

Someone can obtain the economically useful product of years of human work far more cheaply than recreating that knowledge independently.

When the affected party is a novelist, this may be described as transformative learning.

When the affected party owns a frontier model, it becomes industrial extraction.

The similarity does not prove legal equivalence. Models involve contractual access, authentication systems, trade secrets and security concerns that an openly accessible webpage may not.

But those differences must be articulated, not assumed.

The burden is therefore straightforward:

Identify the principle that makes one form of uncompensated learning legitimate and the other illegitimate, without defining the principle as “because we own the second thing.”

