4. Exhibits From the Output Layer: Apparently Copyright Is a Vibe

The examples accompanying this paper make the asymmetry more visually obvious.

The supplied material includes claimed AI-generated or AI-assisted outputs involving:

* a Sonic game environment and character;
* a Pokémon battle application containing recognisable Pokémon characters;
* an AI-built Superman scene explicitly requested to resemble a Zack Snyder film;
* a browser-playable recreation of Grand Theft Auto: Vice City;
* a comic using recognisable Powerpuff Girls characters and visual language;
* an OpenAI-themed comic using recognisable Beavis and Butt-Head characters;
* another comic using recognisable Ren & Stimpy characters.

These screenshots do not prove that any particular copyrighted image was contained in a training set. Nor, standing alone, do they establish legal infringement.

They demonstrate something narrower but important:

the product layer is plainly capable of producing highly recognisable expressions based on valuable third-party franchises and characters.

That matters because modern AI companies are not merely passive research laboratories accidentally discovering copyright law.

They operate commercial systems capable of generating material that competes in cultural and creative markets while simultaneously telling users that infringement of third-party rights is prohibited.

OpenAI’s own terms require users not to violate other people’s rights. Its current API terms even include an IP indemnity for some third-party infringement claims arising from model output, subject to important exclusions. 

In other words, the company recognises that output-side IP risk is real enough to:

1. regulate contractually,
2. allocate liability for it,
3. build filtering systems around it,
4. and sell enterprise assurances concerning it.

This is not a mysterious problem nobody has noticed.

It is a commercial risk already priced into the product.

