5. The Pirated Library Problem

The Anthropic litigation provides an unusually clean example of why acquisition method matters.

In 2025, the court distinguished between:

* training a model on lawfully acquired copyrighted books, which it considered capable of constituting fair use;
* and maintaining a library acquired through piracy.

The latter created separate copyright exposure, culminating in a settlement approved in July 2026 worth approximately $1.5 billion. 

This distinction is devastating to simplistic rhetoric from either side.

It means:

“AI training is always theft”

is too broad.

But so is:

“Training is transformative, therefore provenance doesn’t matter.”

Apparently provenance matters enormously when the source copy itself was unlawfully acquired.

That leads naturally to the principle this paper proposes:

The Lawful Input Principle

If an AI developer wishes to claim legal privileges associated with transformative machine learning, it should first be able to establish that the material entered the pipeline through a legally defensible channel.

Transformation should not operate as a metaphysical washing machine into which unlawfully acquired material enters at one end and emerges as innocent mathematics at the other.

