6. The Transparency Asymmetry

Creators face another problem that frontier laboratories generally do not: they frequently cannot determine whether their work was used at all.

The UK Government’s 2026 report identified this explicitly as an information asymmetry. Developers know what they collected. Rights holders frequently do not know whether their work was included, where it was obtained, or whether they have a viable claim. 

That asymmetry radically alters enforcement.

Suppose an author believes a model may have consumed her novel.

To investigate she may need:

* knowledge of training datasets;
* crawler logs;
* acquisition dates;
* dataset lineage;
* model-development documentation;
* potentially expensive litigation merely to obtain discovery.

Now invert the relationship.

Suppose a competitor generates millions of queries against Claude to train another model.

Anthropic can see:

* the accounts;
* the request volume;
* the output;
* the traffic pattern;
* the geographical origin;
* the authentication method;
* the suspected extraction campaign.

It can identify the behaviour, block the accounts and publicly describe the activity as illicit. 

One side possesses telemetry.

The other possesses suspicions.

This makes the argument that creators should simply “enforce their existing rights” rather hollow when the architecture prevents them from determining whether those rights have been implicated in the first place.

