7. The Infrastructure Protection Paradox

We can now formalise the paradox.

Let:

H = \text{human-created informational capital}

and

M = \text{model-created informational capital}.

The permissive AI-training position frequently treats transformation

H \rightarrow M

as socially beneficial because the resulting model does not merely store the original works but learns statistical structure from them.

Now consider a competitor performing:

M \rightarrow M_2

through extensive output distillation.

The competitor can make an almost identical argument:

* it does not merely copy outputs;
* it extracts statistical behaviour;
* it learns patterns;
* the resulting model has different weights;
* it produces new outputs;
* its model is therefore transformative.

Yet frontier laboratories generally reject this argument when the input object is their own model.

That does not mean they are legally wrong. Contract violation, circumvention, fraudulent accounts, trade-secret law and cybersecurity can produce materially different legal results.

It means the conceptual justification changes precisely at the boundary of ownership.

Hence the Infrastructure Protection Paradox:

Statistical abstraction is invoked as a defence against property claims downstream from human creators, while statistical abstraction is treated as appropriation when performed downstream from frontier models.

A theory of property that changes depending on whose server rack contains the valuable object is not yet a theory.

It is a business model.

