8. Regulation Enters the Chat

The paradox becomes more interesting when frontier laboratories ask governments to regulate the industry.

OpenAI announced in September 2026 that it was pushing for mandatory national AI safety requirements, including capability-based regulation and support for independent assessment infrastructure. 

There may be perfectly serious safety reasons for such rules.

But regulation also has an unavoidable industrial effect.

Mandatory audits, cybersecurity controls, incident reporting, safety evaluations, lawyers, compliance departments and certification infrastructure create fixed costs.

An incumbent with billions in revenue, existing safety teams and established governmental relationships can spread those fixed costs across a huge customer base.

A new entrant cannot.

The mechanism is elementary:

\text{Mandatory compliance}
\rightarrow
\text{fixed cost}
\rightarrow
\text{higher entry threshold}
\rightarrow
\text{reduced challenger advantage}.

This does not establish improper motive.

It establishes an economic consequence.

And the consequence becomes particularly important when the challenger is a Chinese laboratory attempting to compete through dramatically lower training or inference cost.

If a regulatory system forces the challenger to reproduce the expensive institutional layer already maintained by American incumbents, then regulation functions — intentionally or otherwise — as a competitive moat.

Safety regulation can therefore be both:

* genuinely motivated by risk;
* and commercially protective of incumbents.

There is no logical contradiction between those propositions.

