10. “But It Was Publicly Accessible”

One of the weakest moral arguments in this debate is that publicly accessible information is therefore available for unrestricted computational appropriation.

The UK Government’s analysis directly rejects that simplification: publicly available material online may still be protected by copyright, and weak technical exclusion mechanisms can create a market failure because right holders struggle to prevent copying or obtain remuneration. 

Public accessibility is not equivalent to abandonment of rights.

A painting displayed in a gallery is publicly accessible.

A book placed in a library is publicly accessible.

A website is publicly accessible.

None of those facts independently transfers ownership.

The internet unfortunately created a cultural assumption that machine readability equals ownerlessness.

AI scaled that assumption from search indexing to industrial production.

