12. Do AI Companies Have a Right to Protection?

Legally: yes.

Even a company found to have violated somebody else’s copyright does not lose all rights to its own property.

There is no coherent legal principle saying:

“You infringed copyright once, therefore everyone may now steal your servers.”

Model weights may constitute trade secrets. Infrastructure can be protected by computer-misuse law, contract, confidentiality, cybersecurity law, copyright in software and other legal doctrines.

The serious challenge is therefore not forfeiture.

It is reciprocity.

AI firms should be entitled to protection.

But they should have to explain why creators deserve materially weaker protection against the same underlying economic phenomenon:

another party using your expensive intellectual work as an input to build something cheaper that competes with you.

If the answer is “because ours is technically different,” specify exactly which difference matters.

If the answer is “because our terms forbid it,” explain why website terms, rights reservations and crawler exclusions should not receive comparable respect.

If the answer is “because model extraction damages our market,” acknowledge that market substitution is precisely one of the harms creators allege from generative AI.

If the answer is “because we invested billions,” remember that human culture did not materialise free of investment either.

