13. A Reciprocity Standard

The solution is not to prohibit machine learning.

It is to stop operating two theories of property simultaneously.

A coherent regime could contain several principles.

13.1 Lawful acquisition

Training safe harbours should depend upon legally defensible acquisition of source material.

No “pirated first, transform later” defence.

13.2 Meaningful provenance transparency

Developers need not publish proprietary recipes, but independent regulators should be capable of auditing major training sources.

A creator cannot exercise rights against a process they are prohibited from seeing.

13.3 Machine-readable rights reservations that actually work

If AI firms expect their API terms, anti-bot measures and regional restrictions to be respected, they should support reliable technical systems through which creators can reserve equivalent rights.

13.4 Symmetrical market-harm analysis

If extracting Claude’s capabilities cheaply harms Anthropic because a rival can avoid the original development cost, then the same economic concept should at least be considered when AI substitutes for books, art, software or journalism used to build it.

13.5 Competition review of AI safety rules

Every major AI regulation should include an assessment of whether its compliance costs disproportionately entrench incumbent laboratories.

Safety regulation should protect society, not quietly become the world’s most expensive bouncer.

13.6 Output-side responsibility

When systems can readily generate recognisable commercial characters, franchises and derivative media, providers cannot plausibly treat all resulting IP risk as an unforeseeable eccentricity of users.

The examples supplied with this paper make that proposition difficult to take seriously.

