15. The “Innovation” Escape Hatch

The predictable response is that restricting training would inhibit innovation.

Certainly it might.

Licensing costs money.

Provenance costs money.

Auditing costs money.

Paying people generally costs more than not paying them.

But “it would make my business more expensive” is not normally a complete response to a property-right claim.

Indeed, when Chinese competitors allegedly distilled Claude at lower cost, Anthropic’s objection was essentially that they could acquire expensive capabilities without bearing the full original development expense. 

The creator’s answer writes itself:

Yes. We know the feeling.

⸻

16. The Strange Case of the Protected Copier

The modern frontier AI company increasingly occupies four positions simultaneously:

As collector:
Public information should remain available for machine learning.

As defendant:
Training is transformative and socially beneficial.

As property owner:
Model extraction, reverse engineering and competitive distillation must be prohibited.

As lobbyist:
Government should establish mandatory safety and security frameworks around frontier AI.

None of those positions is independently absurd.

Together they require considerably more philosophical work than the industry has so far supplied.

Without that work, the apparent rule becomes:

Copy upward, regulate downward.

