17. Conclusion: Please Respect the Property Sign on the Data Centre

This paper does not argue that model weights should be stolen.

It argues something considerably more inconvenient:

AI companies are right that valuable intellectual infrastructure deserves protection.

They have simply arrived at that insight remarkably late — approximately at the moment the valuable intellectual infrastructure became theirs.

The correct response is therefore not to remove protection from AI laboratories.

It is to apply the principle consistently.

If provenance matters for model extraction, provenance matters for training data.

If unauthorised economic appropriation matters when a competitor distils Claude, it deserves consideration when a model absorbs human-created work.

If terms governing automated extraction deserve respect, machine-readable creator reservations deserve respect.

If investments of billions create legitimate expectations of protection, investments of entire careers do not become worthless because they are distributed across millions of individual creators.

And if AI companies want governments to protect the extraordinary intellectual assets built inside their data centres, they should perhaps avoid constructing a legal philosophy in which property rights mysteriously become sacred only after crossing the data-centre threshold.

The proposition is not anti-AI.

It is simpler:

You may have intellectual property.
So may everybody else.

Or, translated into the current industrial dialect:

Scraping is learning.
Distillation is theft.
Copyright is complicated.
Model weights are sacred.
Please enjoy the future responsibly.

